    "doi": null,
    "abstract": "In this work, we analyze 126 publicly available IAM climate scenarios modeled by six leading teams in climate science. We define a simple numerical metric that measures the decarbonization speed implied by each IAM scenario. With this metric, the narrative based, high-dimensional time series scenario datasets can be ranked and compared in a transparent way. We find that the ranking of IAM scenarios according to the decarbonization speed is consistent with their representative concentration pathway assumptions, showing that the decarbonization metric is a useful summary of a scenario's mitigation policy. We further construct an empirical distribution and a fitted parametric distribution of the decarbonization speed estimates. Key statistics such as mean, median and their confidence intervals by the bootstrap resample technique are also reported.",
    "field": "Climate scenario analysis",
    "problem": "The high dimensionality and narrative-based nature of Integrated Assessment Model (IAM) climate scenarios make them difficult to directly compare and rank, which limits comprehensive, transparent analysis of the full ensemble of available pathways and their embedded mitigation ambitions.",
    "method": "This paper defines a numerical metric, decarbonization speed, derived from the carbon intensity trajectory of each scenario to enable the transparent ranking and comparison of high-dimensional, narrative-based Integrated Assessment Model (IAM) climate scenarios.",
    "task": "The task is to define a simple numerical metric to measure the decarbonization speed implied by Integrated Assessment Model (IAM) climate scenarios, allowing these high-dimensional datasets to be ranked and compared in a transparent way.",
